{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788133065,"nonce":"Jou7Hal11bU7joUeHeBCFOdONTw=","message":"PARKED — opus worker stopped at the session budget line, not for any problem\nwith the spec. Back to `state:next`; nothing here is blocked. Orchestrator,\nsession 4, dorian's Linux box.\n\n**It left nothing behind:** no commits, worktree removed, branch pointer deleted.\nIt had begun `Source/AutoRimmer/PawnSerializer.cs` (349 lines, uncommitted, never\nrun) and that file is gone deliberately — a partly-written, unreviewed serializer\nwould mislead the next worker more than it would help, which is the same call\nsession 2 made.\n\n**But its research is preserved below, because it is the most valuable thing the\nrun produced and re-deriving it would cost another opus session.** This is a\nhazard catalogue for pawn serialization, and it found several LAZY-INIT-WRITE\ntraps that are NOT in 2.1's exemplar — cases where merely *reading* a pawn\nmutates game state. `StatDef.GetValue` writing a cache on every call with no\nread-only mode is the one to internalise: it means \"observers never mutate\" is\nnot achievable by care alone on that path, and the spec has to decide what to do\nabout it.\n\nTreat it as leads to VERIFY against the decompiled source at\n`rimworld-tools/Info/decompiled/RimWorldBase/`, not as established fact — it was\nwritten by an agent that never got to run its code, and it has not been reviewed\nby me. The file:line citations are its own.\n\n--- verbatim from the abandoned file's header ---\n\n    // `c.Fogged(map)` and reports the count it skipped. Nothing here is dev:*.\n    //\n    // ===================== MUTATION / COST HAZARDS ==========================\n    // This is the largest observer surface in the project, and \"read-only\" is\n    // NOT the same as \"a plain field read\". Everything below was checked\n    // against the decompiled 1.6 tree (rimworld-tools/Info/decompiled/\n    // RimWorldBase). Findings, worst first:\n    //\n    //  * LAZY-INIT WRITE — the policy trackers. Pawn_OutfitTracker.\n    //    CurrentApparelPolicy (RimWorld/Pawn_OutfitTracker.cs:13),\n    //    Pawn_FoodRestrictionTracker.CurrentFoodPolicy (:14) and\n    //    Pawn_DrugPolicyTracker.CurrentPolicy (:18) all do\n    //      if (curPolicy == null) curPolicy = \u003cdatabase\u003e.Default…();\n    //    inside the GETTER. Reading one on a pawn that never had a policy — a\n    //    raider, a visitor, a freshly generated prisoner — permanently assigns\n    //    it and the assignment is scribed into the save. That is the\n    //    _mp/DETERMINISM.md lazy-getter hazard in its purest form: an observer\n    //    that changes the save by looking. We read the PRIVATE BACKING FIELDS\n    //    instead (AccessTools refs below, the AlertScanner.activeAlerts\n    //    pattern) and report null as null.\n    //\n    //  * RED ERROR + WRITE — Pawn_WorkSettings.GetPriority (RimWorld/\n    //    Pawn_WorkSettings.cs:160) calls ConfirmInitializedDebug(), which on an\n    //    uninitialised pawn does `Log.Error(...)` AND EnableAndInitialize()\n    //    (:131). Any pawn whose work settings were never turned on — most\n    //    non-colonists — would both breach the standing zero-red-errors\n    //    invariant and gain work settings from being looked at. GUARD is\n    //    `pawn.workSettings.EverWork` (:28, == priorities != null); the work\n    //    section returns `{\"initialized\": false}` and calls nothing.\n    //\n    //  * WRITE (bounded) — Pawn_RelationsTracker.OpinionOf →\n    //    ThoughtHandler.TotalOpinionOffset → SituationalThoughtHandler.\n    //    AppendSocialThoughts → CheckRecalculateSocialThoughts, which ADDS an\n    //    entry to the pawn's cachedSocialThoughts dictionary for the queried\n    //    pawn and stamps lastQueryTick (RimWorld/SituationalThoughtHandler.cs:\n    //    142-193). Querying an opinion therefore allocates situational social\n    //    thoughts for that pair. Self-healing — RemoveExpiredThoughtsFromCache\n    //    drops entries 300 ticks after their last query — but while the game is\n    //    PAUSED nothing ticks, so entries we create sit until time runs again.\n    //    Hence OpinionQueryCap: the number of pairs one call may perturb is\n    //    bounded and published as `relations.queried`.\n    //\n    //  * WRITE (idempotent) — Thing.GetStatValue is not free. StatWorker.\n    //    GetValue writes temporaryStatCache/immutableStatCache on EVERY call\n    //    (RimWorld/StatWorker.cs:34-67) and the write is not gated on the\n    //    cacheStaleAfterTicks argument — there is no read-only mode. The\n    //    dictionaries are NOT concurrent, which is an independent proof of the\n    //    project's main-thread rule: GetStatValue off-thread corrupts them.\n    //    Thing.MaxHitPoints IS a GetStatValue call (Verse/Thing.cs:105).\n    //\n    //  * LAZY CACHE FILL (safe, but not free) — SkillRecord.Level →\n    //    GetLevel() → TotallyDisabled/PermanentlyDisabled/Aptitude, three\n    //    separate BoolUnknown/int? caches populated on first read (RimWorld/\n    //    SkillRecord.cs:82/97/141). Stable and correct; the game dirties them\n    //    on the right events. Also HediffSet.GetMissingPartsCommonAncestors()\n    //    (Verse/HediffSet.cs:936) fills and returns THE cached list.\n    //\n    //  * COST — Room.Role is a full lazy room analysis (see DigestVerb's\n    //    header). It is called at most ONCE per `pawn \u003cid\u003e` call here and\n    //    NEVER in the `pawns` list, which is the hot one.\n    //\n    // ========================= FIELD DOCS ==================================\n    // `skills[].level` is GetLevelForUI() — the number on the Character tab,\n    // which zeroes only for a PERMANENTLY disabled skill. `level_raw` is the\n    // stored levelInt, before Aptitude and before either disable rule; they\n    // differ on a pawn with skill-affecting genes or a temporary disable.\n    //\n    // `apparel[].wear` uses the game's own thresholds, not ours:\n    // ThoughtWorker_ApparelDamaged (RimWorld/ThoughtWorker_ApparelDamaged.cs)\n    // is `frayed` below 0.5 and `tattered` below 0.2 of MaxHitPoints, counting\n    // only apparel with def.useHitPoints, not locked, and careIfDamaged. HP is\n    // omitted entirely for !useHitPoints things, whose HitPoints field is the\n    // uninitialised -1 (Verse/Thing.cs:37) while MaxHitPoints still returns a\n    // plausible stat — a silently wrong ratio if you divide them anyway.\n    //\n    // `health.capacities` is PawnCapacityUtility.CalculateCapacityLevel — the\n    // number the health tab PRINTS (RimWorld/HealthCardUtility.cs:1031 uses the\n    // cached capacities.GetLevel only to pick the label colour). It is a pure\n    // function of the hediff set and writes no cache.\n    //\n    // `mood.thoughts` is the game's own grouping (ThoughtHandler.\n    // GetDistinctMoodThoughtGroups + MoodOffsetOfGroup) filtered by\n    // VisibleInNeedsTab, exactly as PawnNeedsUIUtility.\n    // GetThoughtGroupsInDisplayOrder does — with two deliberate deviations: we\n    // do NOT write Thought.cachedMoodOffsetOfGroup (the game's sort scratch\n    // field; observers do not write), and we tie-break on the label rather than\n    // GetHashCode(), which is not stable run to run and would break rwtest.\n    //\n    // Single-map by design (v1): callers pass Find.CurrentMap.\n    public static class PawnSerializer\n    {\n\n--- end verbatim ---\n\nEverything in the dispatch comment above still stands: worktree isolation,\nworker never launches the game, no DLL commits, orchestrator runs all in-game\nacceptance and owns the `Build:` commit. 2.6's corrections to the exemplar are\nmerged, so the do-not-copy list in comment 4 is discharged.","files":null}]}